try_lock, on each of Boost.Fiber's four mutexes, takes the mutex under the
wait queue's spinlock if it is free, lets the spinlock go, yields, and only
then reads owner_ to say whether it got it. A lock() or unlock() on another
thread writes owner_ under that spinlock meanwhile, so the read is a data
race, which ThreadSanitizer reports wherever a thread try_locks a fiber mutex
another thread uses. The answer is the same either way: only the owner
clears owner_, and a fiber that does not hold the mutex is in no wait queue
to be handed it, so whether this fiber holds it cannot change across the
yield. It is read under the spinlock instead. Boost's develop and master
still read it after.

diff --git a/src/mutex.cpp b/src/mutex.cpp
index 7b06451..3b0b718 100644
--- a/src/mutex.cpp
+++ b/src/mutex.cpp
@@ -53,10 +53,14 @@ mutex::try_lock() {
     if ( nullptr == owner_) {
         owner_ = active_ctx;
     }
+    // whether this fiber holds the mutex is read under the spinlock: only
+    // the owner clears owner_, so the answer holds across the yield, and
+    // a read after it would race another thread's lock() or unlock()
+    bool const locked = active_ctx == owner_;
     lk.unlock();
     // let other fiber release the lock
     active_ctx->yield();
-    return active_ctx == owner_;
+    return locked;
 }
 
 void
